# Documentation

Documentation ships **in the same PR as the code it describes**. Stale docs are defects,
fixed with the change that made them stale.

## Wiki structure

All developer documentation lives under `docs/`, organized by category — only
`docs/README.md` sits at the root as the navigation home:

| Category | Path |
|---|---|
| Policy | `docs/policy/` |
| Architecture | `docs/architecture/` |
| Design System | `docs/design-system/` |
| Features | `docs/features/{domain}/` |
| Platform (iOS/Android/Web specifics) | `docs/platform/` |
| CI / release | `docs/ci/` |
| Storybook | `docs/storybook/` |

Each category folder has an `Index.md` linking its contents; new categories create the
folder + `Index.md` and update the parent navigation in the same PR.

## Placement rules

- New design-system component → `docs/design-system/components/{category}/<name>.md`,
  linked from the design-system index.
- New store, HTTP surface, or linking behavior → `docs/architecture/`.
- New product domain → `docs/features/{domain}/`.
- Security-relevant decisions (pinning setup, storage tiers, auth flow) →
  `docs/architecture/` with the threat reasoning recorded.

## Same-PR requirements

- Every new public surface (component, store, screen flow, script) ships with its doc.
- Docs are written in **English prose**; i18n keys are quoted, translated strings are not.
- Index files and cross-links are updated when files move — broken links are review
  blockers.

## Rulebook sync

The `rules/` folder is the binding policy summary. When a policy doc under `docs/policy/`
changes materially, the corresponding rule file under `rules/` is updated **in the same
PR**, including the index (`rules/00-rules-index.mdc`) when the structure changes.
Rules summarize; wikis carry full detail, tables, and examples. If they conflict, the
source doc wins — fix the rule file.

## Writing for humans and LLMs

- Every rule states **what** is required, **what** is forbidden, and **how to verify**.
- Prefer tables and short imperative sentences over long prose.
- Never rely on tribal knowledge: name the file paths, commands, and thresholds
  explicitly so an agent with no prior context can comply.
